-- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC001 The plugin opens every turn through the server mint operation (`POST /qbrainai/sessionlog/{agent}/{sessionId}/mint` with an `ingressNonce`, or its client and REPL equivalents, called only by hooks and adapters);
the server mints `req-<server UTC yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>-<8 lowercase hex random>`, opens the turn `in_progress`, and returns the token;
the plugin never invents a requestId. A minted requestId is unique within the workspace, enforced by the database for minted turns across all sessions;
a violation of that requestId index makes the server generate a new requestId (at most 5 retries after the original attempt, 6 attempts in all, then 500 `mint_collision_exhausted`) and never merges into another turn, while a violation of the nonce index means the same event and returns the existing requestId (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC002) (`MinterCollision_ReturnsDistinctId`, `MintCollision_ConcurrentCrossSession_DistinctIds` on SQLite, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server).  [A1 server, A3 plugin +A8]
-- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC002 Repeating the mint with the same agent, MCP session id, and `ingressNonce` (network retry, two hook processes started for one event, or failsafe replay after an outage) returns the same requestId and opens no second turn (server behavior on every host;
on copilot and classic cline the hook applies the equal-field rule to a `merged` result of a new invocation, so it fails the host session closed instead of binding, while a stored mint retry or failsafe drain binds normally). A same-nonce mint for a turn that is already terminal returns `merged` with that `storedStatus`, leaves the status unchanged, and the hook neither makes it the write target nor adds it to the open list or the stop ledger (`Mint_SameNonceTerminalTurn_MergedNotReopened` server side;
`PromptHook_SameNonceTerminalTurn_Merged_NotTargetNotOpenNotLedger` plugin side). [A1 server, A3 plugin, A4 plugin write target, open list, and ledger +A8]
-- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC003 Two user messages with identical prompt text (an intentional repeat, at any interval;
on copilot and classic cline the equal-field rule governs a repeat that also shares the timestamp) are two events, derive two nonces, and receive two requestIds and two turns;
two independent hook processes receiving one event (a doubly registered hook) derive one nonce from the host binding table and receive one requestId on every host whose nonce is a stable per-message id (codex, grok, grok-bot, cline-v2, opencode;
on bridge hosts the single bridge per host session mints, TR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001 AC-006);
on copilot and classic cline a second registration is refused before any mint (D11 and the classic-cline single-registration check), so the positive two-process case does not apply there. On copilot and classic cline, whose nonce hashes event fields with no per-message id, two messages whose timestamps differ mint two turns, a second new invocation with equal session, timestamp, and prompt cannot be told apart from a separate message, so it mints nothing and the host session fails closed under D6 (`indistinguishable_arrival`) rather than merging or guessing, and a retry or failsafe replay of one stored mint reuses its nonce (`Copilot_SamePromptDifferentTimestamp_TwoMints`, `Copilot_EqualFieldsSecondInvocation_FailsClosed_NoSecondMint`, `Copilot_MintRetryAndFailsafeReplay_ReuseNonce_NoFailClose`, `Cline_SamePromptDifferentTimestamp_TwoMints`, `Cline_EqualFieldsSecondInvocation_FailsClosed_NoSecondMint`, `Cline_MintRetryAndFailsafeReplay_ReuseNonce_NoFailClose`). [A1 server, A3 plugin, A8 Node hosts +A4 +A9]
