s filed under) and with the main workspace, and record both results (see \"Risks and incidental bugs\"). Record the approval HEAD (`git rev-parse HEAD`) and confirm `git diff --name-only f56dcf70..HEAD` still touches no `src/`, `tests/`, `build/`, or `plugins/` path (otherwise re-verify every source anchor in this plan before continuing). Save this plan as `docs/plans/PLAN-SESSIONTURN-SESSIONREVIEW-001.md`, including the wire contracts below.",
    "2. Read-only spike (P0.2 writes nothing to the repo or the stores beyond its probe receipts, recorded payloads, and its PlanningDecision; the temporary codex observer files of (c) live outside the repo and are deleted at its end), recorded as a PlanningDecision: confirm from the session transcript and server turns how `<task-notification>` and slash commands arrive at UserPromptSubmit and whether each produces a `queue-operation` `enqueue` record; capture a Claude Code transcript excerpt with `queue-operation`/`enqueue` lines as the A3 arrival-bridge fixture (the bridge design does not depend on whether UserPromptSubmit also fires for queued messages, because on bridge hosts the prompt hook neither mints nor claims); and run every probe the host binding table assigns to P0.2, each recorded with its receipt, an outcome of pass, fail, or unavailable (host not installed or not reachable), and the resulting D6/D7 capability disposition (P0.8 writes it into `docs/AGENT-PLUGIN-FEATURE-MATRIX.md` from these receipts): (a) claude-code: whether every user prompt, in each prompt form named in FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC009, produces a transcript `enqueue` record, and whether `dequeue` always takes the head of the queue; (b) claude-cowork: whether Cowork executes plugin hooks, and the same bridge probes as (a) (every prompt form produces an `enqueue` record, `dequeue` always takes the head); Cowork is supported only if all pass, otherwise `turn_logging_unsupported` (D6) with its feature-matrix row; (c) codex, observed in an isolated probe home, because the codex plugin registers no hooks before A9 (agent plugin matrix) and nothing of the operator's own Codex setup (`~/.codex` and the shared app-server daemon that hosts its interactive sessions) may change: P0.2 records the SHA-256 of `~/.codex/config.toml`, then creates `/tmp/qbrain-p02-codex-<utc>/` holding `observer.ps1` (it reads the hook's stdin JSON and appends `{ observedAtUtc, payload }`, built from native objects with `ConvertTo-Json -Compress`, as one line to `observed.jsonl` in that directory, writes nothing to stdout, and exits 0) and `home/config.toml`. `home` is the `CODEX_HOME` of every probe command (codex-cli 0.160.1 reads its config and auth from `CODEX_HOME`); every probe command sets it with `env` from PowerShell, as the deploy step sets `PATH`; its config sets `approval_policy = \"never\"`, installs no plugin, and sets `enabled = false` (the `[plugins.\"<name>\"]` key of the operator's own `config.toml`, not in the help output) for every bundled plugin whose manifest declares hooks (on 2026-10-10 `browser@openai-bundled`, `chrome@openai-bundled`, and `unified-computer-use@openai-bundled`), so the probe writes nothing to the MCP stores and the observer is the only enabled hook, and registers inline command hooks for SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit, PostToolUse, and Stop (no matcher) that run the observer with `pwsh -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File` (Codex hooks documentation, https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/hooks: inline `[hooks]` tables in a config layer; a non-managed hook runs only once its definition is trusted). The operator signs in inside the probe home once (`env CODEX_HOME=<home> codex login`; the agent never handles the credentials). Run 1 (scripted): `env CODEX_HOME=<home> codex exec --dangerously-bypass-hook-trust -s read-only -C /home/sharpninja/github/McpServer --json \"Run the shell command printenv CODEX_THREAD_ID and reply with its output only.\"`, stdout kept; it passes when the observer recorded a UserPromptSubmit line whose payload carries a nonempty `session_id` and `turn_id`, and it records whether the printed `CODEX_THREAD_ID` equals that `session_id` (the `CODEX_THREAD_ID` verb key of the host binding table is selected only when the same equality also holds in run 2's app-server-hosted session, otherwise the live-session rule). Run 2 (the operator at the keyboard) hosts an interactive session on a private `codex app-server` started only for the